Workflow Explanation
1.	Upload inputs: The teacher uploads one question paper PDF, one answer-key PDF, and multiple separate student answer-sheet PDFs.
2.	Treat each PDF independently: Each student PDF is treated as one student submission. A student PDF may contain multiple pages.
3.	Read every page: Selectable digital text is extracted locally. Scanned or handwritten pages are sent to Vision OCR.
4.	Combine the student submission: All page transcriptions belonging to the same student are combined before grading.
5.	Identify the student: The system extracts the student name and roll number when available.
6.	Grade the submission: The grading model compares the student's answers with the question paper and answer key.
7.	Calculate results: The system produces score, percentage, grade, confidence, feedback, and review status.
8.	Continue the batch: An individual paper failure is captured as an error/review item instead of stopping the complete batch.
9.	Show consolidated results: Processed students appear in a live result table and can be exported.
 
2. Product Requirements Document (PRD)
Document version: Version 4.0 | Hackathon Demo & MVP
3. Product Overview
GradeFlow AI is a generative-AI powered test and paper checking application for teachers. It is designed to process 100+ separate student answer-sheet PDFs in one batch, including scanned or handwritten papers, and produce a consolidated result sheet.
4. Problem Statement
Manual checking of large batches of student papers is repetitive, time-consuming, and difficult to standardize. Scanned answer sheets also make conventional PDF text extraction unreliable.
5. Product Goal
Allow a teacher to upload one question paper PDF and one answer-key PDF.
Allow 100+ separate student answer-sheet PDFs to be uploaded together.
Treat each student PDF as one independent submission.
Use AI vision OCR when papers are scanned or handwritten.
Extract student name and roll number from the paper.
Evaluate answers against the answer key and calculate marks, percentage, grade, and confidence.
Continue processing the batch when an individual paper fails.
Provide a consolidated downloadable Excel result sheet.
Provide teacher review information for low-confidence or failed submissions.
6. Target Users
• School and college teachers
• Exam coordinators
• Tutors and coaching centers
• Hackathon judges evaluating AI education automation
7. User Inputs
• Question Paper: PDF
• Answer Key: PDF
• Student Answer Sheets: multiple separate PDF files, with 100+ supported as a target batch size
8. Core User Workflow
1. Teacher opens the deployed Streamlit application.
2. Teacher uploads the question paper PDF.
3. Teacher uploads the answer key PDF.
4. Teacher selects multiple student answer-sheet PDFs.
5. GradeFlow AI validates the batch and begins processing.
6. Each PDF is processed independently.
7. Text is extracted directly when possible; scanned/handwritten pages use Vision OCR.
8. Student name and roll number are identified.
9. The grading model compares the submission with the question paper and answer key.
10. The application calculates score, percentage, grade, confidence, and review status.
11. Results are combined into a consolidated dashboard and Excel file.
9. Functional Requirements
• Multi-file student upload.
• Per-student processing isolation.
• Page-by-page Vision OCR for scanned/handwritten papers.
• Conservative Vision request sizing to reduce provider rate-limit failures.
• Automatic retry/backoff for transient Groq rate-limit responses.
• Structured AI grading output.
• Name and roll-number extraction.
• Score and percentage calculation.
• Letter grade calculation.
• Confidence score and teacher review flag.
• Batch progress indicator.
• Error capture without stopping the complete batch.
• Excel export and JSON export.
• Teacher review queue and individual student reports.
10. AI / Technical Architecture
Frontend: Streamlit
Programming language: Python
LLM API: Groq
Vision OCR model: configurable Groq vision model
Grading model: configurable Groq text model
PDF text extraction: pypdf
PDF page rendering: PyMuPDF
Data processing: pandas
Excel generation: openpyxl
Deployment: Streamlit Community Cloud
Source control: GitHub
11. Reliability Design
Vision OCR defaults to one page per request to reduce image/input-token pressure.
OCR output is capped to remain below the account's observed output-token limit.
Vision requests are serialized rather than launching many simultaneous vision calls.
Rate-limit errors are retried using a delay derived from the provider response when available.
A single student's failure is recorded as an error/review item rather than terminating the batch.
The application exposes status information so the teacher can identify papers needing review.
