14. Non-Functional Requirements
•	Browser-based workflow; no local Python execution required for the deployed demo.
•	Readable and simple teacher-facing UI.
•	Graceful handling of scanned PDFs.
•	Batch processing must be fault tolerant.
•	Secrets such as the Groq API key must be stored through Streamlit secrets rather than committed to GitHub.
•	The system should clearly communicate that AI-generated grades may require teacher verification.
15. Hackathon Demo Success Criteria
•	Successfully upload question paper and answer key.
•	Successfully upload multiple separate student PDFs.
•	Demonstrate OCR on a scanned/handwritten student paper.
•	Show extracted student name and roll number.
•	Show an automatically generated grade.
•	Show multiple students in one consolidated result table.
•	Download the Excel result sheet.
•	Demonstrate that an error on one paper does not stop the remaining batch.
16. Known Constraints
•	Groq model rate limits can affect throughput for large batches of image-heavy papers.
•	Handwriting quality, scan quality, page orientation, and image resolution affect OCR accuracy.
•	AI grading should be treated as decision support and reviewed where confidence is low.
•	Very large batches may take time because reliability settings intentionally favor conservative Vision requests.
17. Future Enhancements
•	Question-type aware grading for MCQ, short-answer, and long-answer questions.
•	Rubric-based marking.
•	Teacher correction and re-grade workflow.
•	Duplicate/blank paper detection.
•	Class-level performance trends.
•	More export formats.
•	Queue-based background processing for very large batches.
•	Institution-level authentication and role management.
18. MVP Definition
The MVP is complete when a teacher can upload the required PDFs in a browser, process multiple student submissions, obtain student identity and grading results, review exceptions, and download a consolidated result sheet without running the application locally.
